---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CodeExample from "../../components/CodeExample.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import lessonSource from "../../generated/examples/tutorial-multiline-strings.kwe?raw";

const source = lessonSource.trimEnd();
const output = `Ndewo Ụwa!
Ahịrị nke abụọ
Ahịrị nke atọ
otu ahịrị
ahịrị mbụ
ahịrị abụọ`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lesson 9: Multiline strings"
  description="Write one string value that spans several physical lines with three double quotes."
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Tutorial 09"
    title="Multiline strings"
    lead="Keep a single string value readable across many lines, preserve the line breaks, and learn where escapes still apply."
    meta="Beginner · Complete Lesson 8 first"
  />

  <div class="shell content-layout">
    <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Tutorial navigation">
      <a href="/tutorials/standard-library-strings/">← Lesson 8: Standard Library strings</a>
      <a href="/tutorials/multiline-strings/" aria-current="page">
        Lesson 9: Multiline strings
      </a>
    </nav>
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The program</h2>
      <p>Open <code>examples/tutorials/multiline-strings.kwe</code>.</p>
      <CodeExample
        label="examples/tutorials/multiline-strings.kwe"
        code={source}
        output={output}
      />

      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <p>From the repository root, run the entry file:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>node dist/cli/index.js examples/tutorials/multiline-strings.kwe</code></pre>

      <h2>One string, many lines</h2>
      <p>
        A multiline string starts and ends with three double quotes. Everything
        between the delimiters is part of one string value, including the
        physical line breaks:
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>mpempe = """Ndewo Ụwa!
Ahịrị nke abụọ
Ahịrị nke atọ"""</code></pre>
      <p>
        Each <code>bipụta</code> call in the program prints its full value, so
        the stored line breaks appear in the output exactly as written.
      </p>

      <h2>Single line or many</h2>
      <p>
        An ordinary string still uses one pair of double quotes and cannot cross
        a line. A multiline string uses three double quotes and may span many
        lines. Both are the same <code>string</code> type, so either can be
        assigned to a variable and printed.
      </p>

      <h2>Escapes still work</h2>
      <p>
        The five escapes from single-line strings remain available inside a
        multiline string: <code>\n</code>, <code>\t</code>, <code>\r</code>,
        <code>\\</code>, and <code>\"</code>. A literal line break is written
        directly, with no backslash.
      </p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>mpempe = """ahịrị mbụ
ahịrị abụọ"""
bipụta(mpempe)</code></pre>
      <p>
        A single or double quote inside the delimiters is ordinary content; only
        the next unescaped <code>"""</code> ends the string. Comment markers
        such as <code>//</code> inside a multiline string are content, not
        comments.
      </p>

      <h2>Your turn</h2>
      <p>Add these lines and predict the output:</p>
      <pre tabindex="0"><code>okwu = """O kwuru, "Ndewo!"
ahịrị abụọ"""
bipụta(okwu)</code></pre>
      <p>
        Expected output: <code>O kwuru, "Ndewo!"</code> followed by
        <code>ahịrị abụọ</code> on the next line.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout--gold">
        <strong>Common problems</strong>
        Keep the opening and closing <code>"""</code> paired. A string that
        reaches the end of the file without its closing delimiter reports
        lexical error <code>KWE-LEX-006</code>.
      </div>

      <h2>Normative references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/lexical-grammar.md`}>Lexical grammar</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/errors.md`}>Current language errors</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/design-decisions/ADR-030-multiline-strings.md`}>Multiline strings decision</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Next</h2>
      <p>
        Continue with the <a href="/examples/">verified examples</a> and the
        <a href="/docs/language/">language overview</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
